## Rules that are easy to get wrong

- **Tests run the compiled binary** `{{.PluginID}}-plugin`, not your source.
  `dev test` builds first; run `bun test` after it (or after
  `branchkit-cli dev build`), or it tests the old code and can pass.
- **Call the platform through the SDK's typed methods**, e.g.
  `await plugin.collectionAppend({ name, payload })` for the method
  `collection.append`: the method name in camelCase, one request object.
  `reference/specs/plugin-api.md` lists every method's params; the request
  types are exported from `@branchkitdev/plugin-sdk-ts`.
- **`src/actions_gen.ts` is generated** from `action_types` in `plugin.json`.
  Never edit it by hand. After changing `action_types`, regenerate it from this
  directory: `go run github.com/branchkit/branchkit-gen@latest --plugin .`
  (needs Go 1.24 or newer).
- **The plugin is sandboxed** and gets only what `plugin.json` declares:
  privileges under `requires`, the collections it reads under `consumes`,
  network hosts, files. An undeclared call is refused at run time, and the
  tests may not catch it, so declare first: each method in
  `reference/specs/plugin-api.md` names the privilege it needs.
- **Collections this plugin provides are named `plugin.{{.PluginID}}.<name>`.**
  Other names belong to other plugins or the platform.
- **The settings tab uses Datastar 1.0** (`data-on:click`, `data-init`).
  Older forms (`data-on-click`, `data-on-load`) appear in examples on the web
  and silently do nothing here.
- **Log with the SDK**: `await plugin.info("TAG", {...})` (also `debug`, `warn`, `error`).
  Never print to stdout: stdout carries the JSON-RPC messages. Do not log
  the user's own text (what they said, typed, or have on screen).
- **Tests live in `src/index.test.ts`** and run the plugin under
  `branchkit-test-harness`, a real matcher and event bus without the app.
  Simulate a phrase, inject an event, read a HUD channel or a collection:
  see `how-to/debug/test-a-plugin.md`; the rest of the harness API is
  typed in `@branchkitdev/plugin-sdk-ts/harness`.

## When something doesn't work

`dev test` prints one line per check; `!` is a warning, not a failure. A
failing or warning check prints `docs: <page>`, a path under `$D`: read that
page first. `dev test --json` gives the same as JSON.

When the plugin misbehaves in the running app, read the app before changing
code. These commands need the plugin installed and Developer Access turned on
on its card in Settings (ask the user); they change nothing in the app.

1. **Snapshot first.**
   `branchkit-cli dev diagnose {{.PluginID}}` prints the plugin's status,
   recent warnings and errors, its log tail, what it last received, and the
   last chains (`tr_` ids) that touched it, then the commands to run next.
   `--json` gives the same as one object; `--since 10m` narrows the window.
2. **Reproduce without speaking.**
   `branchkit-cli dev say "<phrase>" --simulate` runs the phrase through
   matching and dispatch as if spoken, and records each action instead of
   running it. Never drop `--simulate`: without it the actions really run on
   the user's machine. Its last line is the `dev chain` command for the
   utterance's `tr_` id; run it next.
3. **Follow the chain.**
   `branchkit-cli dev chain <tr_id>` lists the chain's records your plugin
   can see, in order: what matched, what was dispatched, what failed.
4. **Read what the plugin logged and received.**
   `branchkit-cli dev plog {{.PluginID}} --since 60s` (add `--level warn` or
   `--tag GLOB`) is its log. What it received is the replay buffer, the
   "What it received" section of `dev diagnose`. When an expected event never
   arrives, `branchkit-cli dev events --plugin {{.PluginID}} --since 10m`
   and `how-to/debug/debug-event-delivery.md` find where it stopped.
5. **Fix, then prove it without the app.**
   Add a test for the case you reproduced and run `branchkit-cli dev test .`
   and the plugin's own tests. Install into the app again only when the user
   asks.
